  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nUpper level ridge axis sitting right over the region and then\ngoes back southwestward into the Four Corners region. This will\nkeep mostly clear skies across the area through Wednesday \nmorning. Pretty seasonable temperatures will also continue\nacross the area with highs in the upper 70s near Lake Michigan\nand low 80s elsewhere through Wednesday. \n\nTroughing centered over central Canada will begin to push\nsouth and eastward into the upper Midwest and then into the\nGreat Lakes and will increase chances for showers/thunderstorms\nfor mainly the northwestern parts of the CWA by Wednesday\nevening and spread southeastward over the rest of the area \nby early Thursday as a cold front pushes through. The better \nchances for precipitation at this time will be on Thursday with \ngenerally 30 to 60 percent chance of precipitation across the \narea. WPC has our CWA under a marginal chance of exceeding flash\nflood guidance for Wednesday night through Thursday with PWATs\nof around 1.5\" to almost 2\" and surface dew points in the 70s along\nand ahead of the frontal boundary. So will need to watch \ncarefully on how this develops over the next few days. A more \ncyclonic flow to zonal flow then remains over the area well into\nthe weekend. This will bring the potential for periods of \nshowers/thunderstorms especially during the afternoon/early \nevening periods. \n\nRidging then develops over the southeast CONUS by Monday and \nbuilds northward into our area. This will bring warmer and more\nhumid conditions across the region next week.",
